ly looks similar to  how you got your current job you're  going to know better about what roles  are out there and how to interview in  the timeline of interviews so i'm going  to leave you with that because you know  how to do this so you know if you're  chilling like maybe you need to find  time to like rediscover yourself or you  just gotta  recover from some burnout or whatever  that might be you just like need a break  that's that's cool but for me when i'm  like just trying to chill i always need  some sort of backup plan  because  like the world will keep on moving  without you even if you're chilling a  lot of people talk about having like  three months of like living expenses as  a rule of thumb to have in your savings  account um for this kind of chillin  period or even really just as like a  good backup you know because you know i  like i always think of like what's the  worst that could happen like what is  what if i don't feel like going back to  work in three months  or i do feel like going back to work in  three months will i reasonably be able  to find a job so another part of the  calculation for this whole like just  chilling scenario is  like thinking about how reasonably you  could get a job again if you really  needed to in a pinch  because you know what you can't control  what happens in life  and in the world and to you like  so many different things can happen and  sometimes  in that period you're gonna need a job  whether it be for income or for health  insurance or for stability or whatever  i think it's just an important thing to  factor in even if you really don't want  to think about work right now um at the  end of the day we gotta support  ourselves and we have to do what we have  to do in order to survive and thrive so  yeah make sure you leave enough money in  your savings um whenever i left a job  and i was just chilling i made a  spreadsheet you can see it here of all  of my expenses of like what do i need to  do to do life  and just be really realistic about that  and then figure out okay how long do i  want to be chilling  multiply those months times the expenses  and i have how much i should have in my  savings or at least how much i should  have before i  reasonably run out of some sort of  expendable money that i don't mind  spending so yes you know expenses is a  big thing to think about when you're  leaving your job and i know you've  probably thought about this like that's  probably one of the  things that like stuff ain't free  rent isn't free food isn't free so i'm  sure you've thought about it but you  know just in case you have it she wanted  to let you know because  uh it's one thing that really helps you  to be realistic about this whole  quitting thing  and also if you're the type that you're  just like a free spirit and you're fine  with like leaving your job and you  haven't done the math that's fine too  but just so long as you're also willing  to like deal with it like as long as you  take responsibility for your choices  honestly that's fine and also at the end  of the day like your life is yours it's  not mine so like i'm not trying to  dictate  what you should or shouldn't do  it's just if i were to do it this is how  i would think about it all right so the  next thing  healthcare healthcare is heavily tied to  your employment in the united states  and chances are if you're full-time  employed you also got your healthcare  from your employer unless you have a  partner or you're a dependent or  something like that this always depends  on your policy obviously and your  provider and stuff like that but if you  have health care through your employer  it's gonna disappear at some point so a  lot of the times it'll be until the end  of the calendar month that you quit  sometimes to the end of the year i don't  think that's actually super common um i  know for my vision and dental i think  insurance it usually goes until the end  of the calendar year for for like health  medical bills and stuff like that it's  usually to the end of the month so for  health healthcare disappears then how  are you going to get healthcare because  it's important to be insured in case  something happens the u.s department of  labor provides something called cobra  which stands for consolidated omnibus  budget reconciliation act it basically  allows you to continue your health  insurance your current policy your  current provider after you finish or you  quit or you leave a job or something  like that but you have to pay  the insurance out of pocket and  oftentimes it's really really expensive  i think for mine when i left patreon  cobra was like 600 a month or something  like that  and you know that's expensive when  you're not making any income uh at least  it was for me at the time so if you're  someone who has a lot of medical bills  or you suffer ski certain medical bills  or operations or things that need to  come up for me i always take care of my  eyes and so i always think about like  all right well gotta get my annual  checkup on my eyes because they're  really bad and i also gotta get my  contacts and stuff like that i will kind  of plan my leave  around that basically and there's also  other benefits right like not just  health care but also like maybe you get  a gym stipend or you get like commuter  benefits or you get education  stipend or something like that those all  disappear when you leave and so just  make sure you're like taking care of  that  and you're like  good and letting things go or you have  an option to continue them it could also  include things like stock options which  you should definitely check when's the  last day you can buy your options or buy  your stocks or whatever because that can  you know make a difference in your  financial future too i think the last  thing that like i always  really take care of in doing like  whenever i leave a job is to really take  care of my relationships like i don't  want to burn bridges if i don't have to  i guess  because you just never know what's gonna  happen in life you know like you might  do a career switch or you might take a  career fall or you might end up working  with one person you had one interaction  with once  and so your first impression on that  person really matters like so many  different things can happen in life  that i always think about like how can i  leave the most doors open for myself  without  compromise  and also without hurting my chances of  anything per se and so realistically to  me that means when i'm leaving a job i  keep really warm positive relationships  with the people at the company that i'm  leaving so even if like the company that  i'm leaving i'm like not happy with it  it's  usually like not the case that it's the  people that i worked with that i'm  unhappy about per se like directly and  so  my managers and stuff like that i i've  had a really warm relationship with them  my co-workers and stuff too because  here's the thing like if you work in any  industry it tends to be that the  industry is pretty small  and so i feel like reputation is also  kind of important not for like vanity  things but it's just for like future  opportunities how people perceive you is  also going to  result in real life opportunities and  benefits and stuff like that and so  i mean really all it comes down to is  just being nice and respectful and  communicative also because you might  need to go back to that job one day like  you just never know like there's such  thing as like a boomerang job where you  start at a company and then you leave  that company but then you end up  boomeranging back  so if you ever really need that job back  or you really want that job back after  some distance even if you know you're  not gonna go back it's always just good  to keep your options open  and you know the whole thing about like  leaving jobs is like it's a privileged  position to be in you either don't need  a job because you can afford not to have  a job or  you have a lot of different options for  jobs which is also a privileged position  so i think it's important to honor that  that you have and accept that you have  the privilege um and then sometimes just  take that opportunity if you if you want  to and need it and because you can don't  do it just because everyone else is  doing it you know like  that it's it's like you just you have to  make decisions for your own self and  what's best for you because you're the  only person who knows what's best for  you and you have to advocate for  yourself  um i think with the internet and with  information and stuff like that we all  are learning and really  truly learning how different we are from  one another  and how the human experience cannot be  summed into one sentence one paragraph  one book or really anything at all  because we're all just so different from  each other and when we all really  actually don't know each other that well  the only person we can guarantee that we  know well  is ourselves  so  as much as i know it's like  very  easy to go with the crowd and do what  you feel like you want to do because  everyone else is doing it  do it because you want to do it you know  like do it because it's right for you  and your journey and your future  i think that's what like  leaving your job responsibly means  and  you know it's a hard decision to make  i'm not gonna lie about that every one  of my jobs that i left it was very  difficult and i cried a lot  about them either because it was hard or  because i was sad or because i was  frustrated or whatever so yeah you know  just leaving a job is a very personal  decision and i respect whatever you do  at the end of the day and you also will  never quite know if this decision was  the right one so you gotta just trust  your gut trust your instincts really  listen to what you want and then maybe  down the road you'll figure out  how this decision affected others but  you know it'll be okay 